 "beats": [
  {
   "id": "o1",
   "text": "In the 1920s, she was the biggest blues star in America. Then she lay in an unmarked grave for thirty-three years.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "vv1936",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.05
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "hook": "The biggest blues star in America. *Then 33 years in an unmarked grave.*"
   },
   "captions": false
  },
  {
   "id": "o2",
   "text": "She sold records by the hundreds of thousands, at a time when record companies had barely noticed Black buyers.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "close1",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "o3",
   "text": "She faced down the Ku Klux Klan alone. And she made one film. This is it.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar8",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "o4",
   "text": "Her name was Bessie Smith. They called her the Empress of the Blues. This is her story.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Bessie Smith",
    "sub": "the Empress of the Blues"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e1",
   "text": "Bessie Smith was born in Chattanooga, Tennessee, in the early 1890s. Even the year is uncertain.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "young",
    "caption": "Bessie Smith"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "pin": {
     "label": "Chattanooga, Tennessee"
    }
   },
   "section": "early"
  },
  {
   "id": "e2",
   "text": "Her father, William, was a laborer and a part-time Baptist preacher. He died when she was too young to remember him.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Chattanooga",
    "sub": "a poor family"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e3",
   "text": "By the time she was nine, her mother and a brother had died too. Her older sister Viola raised the children.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "bs",
    "caption": "Bessie Smith"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e4",
   "text": "There was no money for school. So Bessie and her brother Andrew went out to the streets.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "vv72",
    "caption": "Bessie Smith"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e5",
   "text": "He played the guitar. She sang and danced, for pennies, on a corner in front of the White Elephant Saloon.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Street corners",
    "sub": "singing for pennies"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r1",
   "text": "In 1904, her oldest brother, Clarence, left home to join a traveling show. He left without telling her, because she would have followed him.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "vv74",
    "caption": "Bessie Smith, photographed by Carl Van Vechten"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1904"
   },
   "section": "road"
  },
  {
   "id": "r2",
   "text": "In 1912 he came back with the show, and got her an audition.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "1912",
    "sub": "the Moses Stokes show"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r3",
   "text": "They hired her as a dancer, because the company already had a star singer: Ma Rainey, the Mother of the Blues.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "rainey",
    "caption": "Ma Rainey, 1923"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r4",
   "text": "People later said Ma Rainey taught Bessie to sing. That is probably a myth. But she likely helped her learn to own a stage, and they stayed friends for life.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "rainey",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r5",
   "text": "By 1913 she had her own act in Atlanta, at the 81 Theater, for about ten dollars a week.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "dance",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1913"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r6",
   "text": "For the next ten years she toured the South, in tent shows and Black theaters, until her name was known all along the East Coast.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "hall",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c1",
   "text": "Then in 1920, another singer, Mamie Smith, no relation, sold a huge number of copies of a record called Crazy Blues.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Crazy Blues",
    "sub": "Mamie Smith, 1920"
   },
   "section": "columbia"
  },
  {
   "id": "c2",
   "text": "The record companies suddenly realized Black Americans would buy records. They went looking for blues women.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "band",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c3",
   "text": "One small Black-owned label, Black Swan, auditioned Bessie, and turned her down. They found her too rough.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Black Swan Records",
    "sub": "too rough"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c4",
   "text": "In February 1923, Columbia Records signed her.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "ad1923",
    "caption": "Columbia Records advertisement, May 1923"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "February 1923"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c5",
   "text": "Her very first record was Downhearted Blues. It sold about 780,000 copies in six months.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "label",
    "caption": "A Bessie Smith record on Columbia"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c6",
   "text": "That June, she married Jack Gee, a security guard from Philadelphia.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "ad2",
    "caption": "Columbia Records advertisement, June 1923"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m1",
   "text": "Over the next ten years, she made about 160 records for Columbia.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "band2",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "section": "empress"
  },
  {
   "id": "m2",
   "text": "She recorded with the best musicians alive, among them Louis Armstrong and Fletcher Henderson. With Armstrong, in 1925, she recorded St. Louis Blues.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "fho",
    "caption": "The Fletcher Henderson Orchestra, 1925, with Louis Armstrong"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m3",
   "text": "Columbia called her the Queen of the Blues. The newspapers promoted her to Empress.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "feathers",
    "caption": "Bessie Smith"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m4",
   "text": "She became the highest-paid Black entertainer of her day, earning up to two thousand dollars a week.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "crowd",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m5",
   "text": "She toured with a show of up to forty performers, and they traveled in her own private railroad car, seventy-two feet long.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Her own railroad car",
    "sub": "72 feet long"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "m6",
   "text": "Her songs were about poor women, prison, drinking, love and freedom. She sang them in a voice that needed no microphone.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar8b",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "k1",
   "text": "In July 1927, her tent show was playing in Concord, North Carolina.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "July 1927",
    "sub": "Concord, North Carolina"
   },
   "section": "klan"
  },
  {
   "id": "k2",
   "text": "During the show, members of her band saw hooded Klansmen outside, pulling up the tent stakes.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "hall2",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "k3",
   "text": "The musicians ran. Bessie did not.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "enter",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "k4",
   "text": "She walked straight out to the hooded men, alone. She cursed them, and told them to leave.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "argue",
    "focus": [
     0.3,
     0.3
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "k5",
   "text": "And they did. Then she went back inside and finished the show.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar3",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "In 1929, the composer W. C. Handy wanted her for a short film built around his most famous song, St. Louis Blues.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "handy",
    "caption": "W. C. Handy"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1929"
   },
   "section": "film"
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "It was filmed in Astoria, in Queens, New York, by the director Dudley Murphy. It was one of the early sound films.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "titlecard",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.5
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.5
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "Bessie played a woman whose man is cheating on her.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "withman",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "f4",
   "text": "He knocks her to the floor and walks out with another woman.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "floor",
    "focus": [
     0.35,
     0.45
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.45
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "f5",
   "text": "So she goes to a bar, and sings her heart out.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "close2",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "f6",
   "text": "It is the only film she ever made, the only time we can see and hear her at once. In 2006, it was added to the National Film Registry.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar12",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "f7",
   "text": "This is Bessie Smith, singing, in 1929.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "close3",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d1",
   "text": "But 1929 was also the year everything turned. She starred in a Broadway musical called Pansy. It flopped, though critics said she was the only good thing in it.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "poster",
    "caption": "St. Louis Blues, 1929"
   },
   "section": "fall"
  },
  {
   "id": "d2",
   "text": "She also left Jack Gee, after his affair with another singer.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "young",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d3",
   "text": "Then the Great Depression hit. People stopped buying records. In 1931, Columbia dropped her.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "1931",
    "sub": "Columbia drops her"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d4",
   "text": "She had to sell her railroad car.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "swing",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d5",
   "text": "In 1933 she made her last records, for a flat fee of thirty-seven dollars and fifty cents a song.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Her last records",
    "sub": "1933"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d6",
   "text": "But she kept singing. With a new partner, an old friend named Richard Morgan, she toured again, and changed her style to fit the new swing music.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "embrace",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "d7",
   "text": "By 1937, people were talking about a comeback.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar13",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x1",
   "text": "On September 26th, 1937, Richard Morgan was driving her down Highway 61 in Mississippi.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "September 26, 1937",
    "sub": "Highway 61, Mississippi"
   },
   "section": "crash"
  },
  {
   "id": "x2",
   "text": "He misjudged the speed of a slow truck ahead of them. Their car hit it side-on, and the roof was torn away.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "The crash",
    "sub": "between Memphis and Clarksdale"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x3",
   "text": "Bessie, in the passenger seat, took the full impact. Her right arm was almost torn off.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "vv75",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x4",
   "text": "A Memphis doctor, Hugh Smith, was first on the scene. He bandaged her arm and sent for an ambulance.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Dr. Hugh Smith",
    "sub": "first on the scene"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x5",
   "text": "She was taken to the G. T. Thomas Afro-American Hospital in Clarksdale. Doctors amputated her arm. She died that morning without waking.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "death",
    "caption": "Bessie Smith's death certificate"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "pin": {
     "label": "Clarksdale, Mississippi"
    }
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x6",
   "text": "Within weeks, a different story spread: that a white hospital had refused to treat her, and she bled to death at its door.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "A story",
    "sub": "that was not true"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "x7",
   "text": "It was not true. The doctor who was there said later that no ambulance in the Deep South would even have tried to take her to a white hospital.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "vv72",
    "caption": "Bessie Smith, 1936"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "g1",
   "text": "In Philadelphia, about ten thousand people filed past her coffin. The funeral home was too small, so her body was moved to a larger hall.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "vv1936",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "October 1937"
   },
   "section": "grave"
  },
  {
   "id": "g2",
   "text": "About seven thousand people came to the funeral.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "close1",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.06,
     1.12
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "g3",
   "text": "She was buried at Mount Lawn Cemetery, outside Philadelphia. But her grave got no headstone.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "No headstone",
    "sub": "Mount Lawn Cemetery, Pennsylvania"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "g4",
   "text": "Her husband, Jack Gee, blocked every attempt to buy one, and reportedly pocketed the money raised for it.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "feathers",
    "caption": "Bessie Smith, 1936"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "g5",
   "text": "Year after year, the Empress of the Blues lay in an unmarked grave.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "close3",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.06,
     1.12
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "j1",
   "text": "Then, in 1970, a young white rock singer heard the story.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "joplin",
    "caption": "Janis Joplin, 1970"
   },
   "section": "joplin"
  },
  {
   "id": "j2",
   "text": "Janis Joplin idolized Bessie Smith. She called her one of the biggest influences on her own singing.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "close2",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.06,
     1.12
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "j3",
   "text": "Janis split the cost of a headstone with Juanita Green, who had done housework for Bessie as a girl.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "Two women",
    "sub": "Janis Joplin and Juanita Green"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "j4",
   "text": "On August 7th, 1970, the stone went up, thirty-three years after the funeral.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "grave",
    "caption": "Bessie Smith's headstone, Mount Lawn Cemetery"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "August 7, 1970"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "j5",
   "text": "It reads: The greatest blues singer in the world will never stop singing.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "grave",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.5
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.1,
     1.2
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "j6",
   "text": "Two months later, Janis Joplin was dead. She was twenty-seven.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "October 1970",
    "sub": "Janis Joplin dies at 27"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l1",
   "text": "In 1989, Bessie Smith was inducted into the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "vv75",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   },
   "section": "legacy"
  },
  {
   "id": "l2",
   "text": "In 2002, Downhearted Blues, her first record, was added to the National Recording Registry.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "label",
    "caption": "Bessie Smith on Columbia"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "l3",
   "text": "And the hospital where she died is now the Riverside Hotel, on the Mississippi Blues Trail.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "The Riverside Hotel",
    "sub": "Clarksdale, Mississippi"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "z1",
   "text": "She came from a street corner in Chattanooga. She became the Empress of the Blues. And for thirty-three years, nobody marked where she lay.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar14",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.35
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1,
     1.06
    ]
   },
   "section": "close"
  },
  {
   "id": "z2",
   "text": "That was Bessie Smith. Subscribe for more.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "endcard"
   },
   "captions": false
  }
 ],
